        "ballot_id": "B044",
        "laureates": [
          "Paul B. Corkum",
          "Maciej Lewenstein"
        ],
        "citation": "For the physical insight and quantum theory of electron recollision in strong laser fields."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B031",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Electron recollision linked the motion of an electron driven by a strong laser field to high-harmonic emission and a route to probing matter on attosecond time scales. Corkum's physical picture and Lewenstein's quantum model gave experiments a framework that has been tested and extended across decades. This is a fundamental advance in how we observe and understand electronic motion, not only an enabling technique.",
    "case_against": "The boundary between this contribution and the wider development of high-harmonic generation and attosecond methods is not sharp. A two-person attribution may omit important contributors to those methods, so the citation must remain focused on recollision and its quantum description.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll regards optical lattice clocks as mature and internationally replicated, while noting that the boundary between foundational ideas and later improvements needs careful assessment.",
        "response": "I agree that the clocks are mature and that the attribution boundary matters. Katori's lattice concept and Ye's complementary advances make a strong case, but the citation would need to identify which original clock contributions merit recognition apart from later gains in precision. That attribution question is substantial, though it does not weaken the evidence for maturity."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson finds the topological-insulator case strong but cautions that its scope spans theoretical ideas, experiments, and later developments that should not be conflated.",
        "response": "That is a fair reservation. The proposed trio gives a clear theory-to-experiment account of the quantum spin Hall effect, but the wider ballot language also reaches the distinct three-dimensional topological-insulator story. I would keep the citation narrowly on the quantum spin Hall prediction and its experimental realization rather than imply that this trio represents every milestone in topological matter."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between Corkum and Lewenstein's contributions and the broader development of high-harmonic and attosecond methods needs careful attribution review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile notes a close collaboration with Anne L'Huillier and coauthored work in adjacent attosecond research. It identifies no direct collaboration with Corkum or Lewenstein; I disclose this proximity for recusal review."
  }
}
